************constructor chaining: 

class A { A() {print a} }
class B extends A { B() {print b} }
class C { C c = new C(); }

its called as constructor chaining as multiple classes are connected with each othe using extends and create the chain
here on c's object creation all constructors are executed in top to bottom nature i,e first A then B then C 

interface doesnt have constructor 

why connecting the two interface uses extends keyword? - search on google

object creation of interface we cant do that becz it doesnt have a constructor so compiler will not know where to start we can create the reference of interface

interface works as rule book for our program and hence its needed in java to set the rules and restrictions

******** IMP *******
